# 🧪 Practical Lab

## Test 1 – Direct UNC Access

From the Windows 11 client:

Press:

```text
Windows + R
```

Enter:

```text
\\SERVER01\Finance
```

The Finance share opened successfully.

This confirmed that:

* Server name resolution was working.
* The SMB share was available.
* The client could communicate with the server.
* The share was accessible using the UNC path.

---

# 🧪 Test 2 – Check SMB Shares

On `SERVER01`, PowerShell can be used to view available SMB shares.

Command:

```powershell
Get-SmbShare
```

To inspect the Finance share:

```powershell
Get-SmbShare -Name Finance
```

This helped verify that the Finance share existed and was published by the server.

---

# 🧪 Test 3 – Check Security Group Membership

To check members of the Finance group:

```powershell
Get-ADGroupMember GG-Finance
```

This can help determine whether the user belongs to the appropriate access group.

---

# 🧪 Test 4 – Check the User

Example:

```powershell
Get-ADUser jfinance
```

This can be used to verify that the user account exists in Active Directory.

---

# 🧪 Test 5 – Test SMB Port

The common SMB port is TCP 445.

From the Windows client:

```powershell
Test-NetConnection SERVER01 -Port 445
```

A successful connection helps confirm that the server is reachable on the SMB port.

---

# 🧪 Test 6 – Mapped Drive

The Finance share was mapped to:

```text
F:
```

with the target:

```text
\\SERVER01\Finance
```

This allowed the Finance folder to be accessed as a normal drive.

